WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.719
Step two counts the notices by kind, across
providers: five advisories, two changes,

2
00:00:05.719 --> 00:00:11.638
six incidents, three maintenance windows,
four retirements and two security notices.

3
00:00:11.638 --> 00:00:15.873
The kind decides the owner, so the
four retirements go to each affected

4
00:00:15.873 --> 00:00:19.962
system's owner, and the two security
notices to security operations.

5
00:00:19.962 --> 00:00:23.150
Step three asks who said
each one needs action.

6
00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:24.450
Eight notices do.

7
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:29.424
Four times the provider said so:
two from AWS's actionability field,

8
00:00:29.424 --> 00:00:35.212
one from Microsoft 365's act-by date, and
one from Oracle's action required type.

9
00:00:35.212 --> 00:00:37.217
And four times a kit rule did:

10
00:00:37.217 --> 00:00:42.710
an AWS scheduled change whose code names
a retirement, the Azure retirement,

11
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:48.203
due at the advisory's end, the Azure
security advisory, which the ISSO reviews,

12
00:00:48.203 --> 00:00:52.650
and a Microsoft 365 post in the
prevent or fix issues category.

13
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.938
The record says which, every time.

14
00:00:54.938 --> 00:00:59.860
And where the provider gave no date,
the kit makes none up: it says no date.
